# PromQL Query

Paste the following query exactly as shown.

```promql
sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{job="auth-service",status=~"5.."}[5m]))
```

Click:

```
Run Queries
```

---

# Understanding the Query

The query filters HTTP requests based on the response status code.

```
status=~"5.."
```

uses a regular expression.

The expression matches every status code beginning with the digit **5**.

Examples include:

```
500 Internal Server Error

501 Not Implemented

502 Bad Gateway

503 Service Unavailable

504 Gateway Timeout
```

Client-side errors such as

```
400

401

403

404
```

are intentionally excluded because they generally represent invalid client requests rather than server failures.

---

# Why Use rate()

The metric

```
http_server_requests_seconds_count
```

is a cumulative counter.

Without `rate()`, the value would increase continuously for the lifetime of the application.

Using:

```promql
rate(metric[5m])
```

calculates how quickly new errors are occurring over the previous five minutes.

This provides a stable and meaningful measurement of the current error rate.

---

# Why Use sum()

As the platform scales horizontally, each Pod maintains its own request counters.

Using:

```promql
sum(rate(...))
```

aggregates all Pods into a single platform-wide error rate.

This ensures the dashboard continues to display a single operational metric regardless of the number of running replicas.

